@*
    AccountShell — the door, for the three screens where nobody is signed in yet.

    Sign-in already solved this layout: a fixed navy panel carrying the seal, and
    ledger paper carrying the one thing to do. Setting a first password, asking
    for a recovery link and choosing a new one are the same person at the same
    door, a step earlier — so they get the same object rather than three pages
    that merely rhyme with it.

    Login.razor deliberately does not use this. Its panel carries the circuit
    field and the scheme terms, which are a pitch to someone deciding whether to
    sign in; nobody arriving here needs to be sold anything, and the one
    orchestrated moment in this console stays on the page that owns it.

    The statement is the panel's whole job, and each page passes the same
    sentence its email led with. Somebody who clicked through from an invitation
    reads the line they just read in their inbox — which is the cheapest proof
    available that this page belongs to that mail.
*@

<div class="account">
    <section class="account__panel">
        <div class="account__lockup">
            <img class="account__seal"
                 src="img/jte-seal.png"
                 width="313"
                 height="320"
                 alt="Jiane's Tech Enterprise, established 2014"
                 fetchpriority="high" />
            <p class="account__division">Solar Fund</p>
        </div>

        @* Statement and note are one block on purpose. The panel is spaced
           apart, so with the note as a third child the statement would ride up
           whenever there was one and drop to the floor whenever there was not —
           and the note comes and goes with the state of the link. Grouped, the
           statement lands in the same place on every screen. *@
        <div class="account__say">
            <h1 class="account__statement">@Statement</h1>

            @if (PanelNote is not null)
            {
                <p class="account__panel-note">@PanelNote</p>
            }
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="account__side">
        <div class="account__form">
            @* h2, not h1: the panel's statement is this page's heading, so a
               second h1 here would leave the document with two. *@
            <h2 class="account__title">@Title</h2>

            @if (Lede is { Length: > 0 })
            {
                <p class="account__lede">@Lede</p>
            }

            @ChildContent
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The panel's one large setting. No word in it is accented.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Statement { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>
    /// The quiet line under the statement — normally the one fact about the link
    /// that the reader needs before they start typing.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? PanelNote { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string Title { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    [Parameter]
    public string? Lede { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
